    "Permutation tests vary package discovery and dependency input order and assert equal decision bytes/digests and stable inclusion-reason order.",
    "Tests demonstrate that same bare package names in different ecosystems remain distinct decision entries."
  ],
  "implementation_notes": [
    "Reuse the existing group, cascade, prerelease, PackageId, Version, and graph structures where their semantics match; introduce a new typed result rather than extending PublishPlan into authority.",
    "Use BTreeMap/BTreeSet or explicitly sorted Vec values at durable boundaries. Do not add Rayon or Tokio: deriving one release decision is policy-sensitive and its deterministic evidence matters more than parallel traversal.",
    "The next spec, SPEC-RELEASE-INTENT-MODEL-009, consumes this result. Git trust, locks, prepared effect inputs, and actual effect dispatch are later dependent specs."
  ]
}
{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-INTENT-MODEL-009",
  "status": "draft",
  "owner_track": "release-security",
  "depends_on": [
    "SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-FOUNDATION-007",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-DECISION-008"
  ],
  "purpose": "Define the typed, serializable, credential-free ReleaseIntentV1 that preserves an approved ReleaseDecisionV1 across process and CI boundaries. The intent is a semantic contract, not a serialized PublishPlan and not a bag of generic fingerprints: it names exact operation identities, semantic inputs, operation prerequisites, and required artifact slots without retaining secrets or mutable prepared inputs.",
  "scope": "Layer-1 durable identity and digest types; typed semantic snapshot projections; registry/tag/forge binding identities; exact operation DAG; artifact-slot declarations; canonical encoding and fail-closed validation. Fresh graph observation, workspace locking, private prepared inputs, state/receipts, provider observation/effects, and CLI/action cutover are intentionally separate successor work.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Placing package discovery, config resolution, URL parsing, workspace access, group solving, or graph validation in callisto-model.",
    "Persisting raw registry endpoints, URL userinfo, query strings, fragments, credentials, HTTP headers, raw configuration bytes, command lines, provider stderr, arbitrary errors, local filesystem paths, or Debug-rendered values in an intent or its digest inputs.",
    "Making ReleaseIntentV1 by itself a capability to publish, tag, push, create a forge release, or reuse a stale prepared input.",
    "Using PublishPlan, PublishReport, PLAN_JSON, a cache entry, or an artifact filename as an operation identity or authorization substitute."
  ],
  "data_model": {
    "intent": "ReleaseIntentV1 is a versioned, serde-deny-unknown-fields record containing IntentDigest, DecisionDigest, source identity, typed semantic snapshot, exact operations, declared artifact slots, and schema/digest scheme versions. It is public/auditable and credential-free.",
    "semantic_snapshot": "ReleaseInputSnapshotV1 is a closed typed projection of facts that affect authorization: qualified package/version/manifest-role facts, effective release-policy facts, relevant dependency-edge facts, typed remote binding identities, source policy facts, and tag/forge policy facts. It must not expose a generic kind:String plus arbitrary fingerprint:String escape hatch.",
    "remote_binding": "RegistryBindingId is a durable identity consisting of a logical registry key and BindingDigest. The graph derives BindingDigest from a validated canonical endpoint binding only after rejecting userinfo, query, fragment, and other credential-bearing forms; the raw endpoint remains graph-private. Distinct normalized endpoint bindings must yield distinct binding identities even where their logical registry key and package/version match. TagBindingId and ForgeBindingId similarly bind their exact tag, target commit, and policy digest rather than a generic display name.",
    "operation": "ReleaseOperationId is exact and role-specific. A registry operation binds qualified package identity, target version, and RegistryBindingId; tag and forge operations bind TagBindingId or ForgeBindingId; an artifact upload binds one declared ArtifactSlotId. It never delegates equality to PackageId::matches or another human-facing wildcard matcher. A forge creation depends on its tag; each artifact upload depends on its forge creation and its own verified slot.",
    "dag": "Every operation has an explicit, duplicate-free prerequisite set of ReleaseOperationId values. The intent stores the canonical stable topological order and rejects cycles, missing prerequisites, self-prerequisites, duplicate operation IDs, and an order inconsistent with prerequisites.",
    "artifact_slot": "ArtifactSlotV1 has a canonical ArtifactSlotId and declares an expected binary artifact role, package/version association where applicable, platform/target identity, intended forge asset name, and a GitHub attestation policy: repository and immutable signer-workflow identity. It does not contain bytes, a path, a token, or an attestation bundle. A later ArtifactManifestV1 binds actual bytes and SHA-256 to this slot, the IntentDigest, and the source commit; the executor separately verifies the GitHub attestation against those bytes and policy. Source registry publishing is a separate operation and never falsely consumes a binary slot.",
    "intent_digest": "IntentDigest is SHA-256 of a versioned, length-prefixed canonical transcript of all authority-bearing typed intent fields. The encoding has explicit field ordering, semantic collection ordering, and length delimiters; it does not depend on JSON object order, skipped serde fields, TOML formatting, raw input-file bytes, platform paths, locale, wall-clock time, or Rust Debug/Ord implementation accidents."
  },
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "ReleaseIntentV1 can be constructed only from a ReleaseDecisionV1 plus explicitly typed semantic observations supplied by the graph layer. It records the exact DecisionDigest and rejects a malformed/unknown schema or an intent whose decision-derived roster, target versions, or declared operations cannot be internally reconciled. Constructing an intent from PublishPlan or a free-form JSON/TOML fingerprint does not satisfy this criterion.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "The durable snapshot uses closed typed projections for every authority-bearing input. Formatting-only changes to configuration do not change its identity, while a semantic release-policy, package/version, dependency, registry-binding, tag-policy, forge-policy, or source-policy change does. No snapshot field may be `kind: String` paired with a generic digest/fingerprint whose input set is undocumented or can include raw bytes.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "RegistryBindingId separates remote targets safely. Graph code rejects a configured endpoint containing userinfo, query, or fragment before constructing a binding. The durable model receives only logical registry identity plus BindingDigest and cannot reconstruct an endpoint. Tests prove that two distinct normalized endpoint bindings for the same logical registry, package, and version produce distinct registry operation identities, while equivalent accepted endpoint spellings produce the same binding identity.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "Intent operation identity is exact and ecosystem-qualified. Two packages with the same bare name but different ecosystems cannot collide; the model rejects duplicate IDs rather than silently coalescing operations. Release-operation comparisons do not call PackageId::matches, whose documented wildcard behavior is reserved for human-facing lookup.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "The operation DAG is complete and deterministic. All prerequisites refer to operations in the same intent, each operation has one deterministic position in a stable Kahn topological order, and every prerequisite precedes its dependent. The model rejects cycles and invalid references before any future executor can act. Tie-breaking is specified by exact operation identity's canonical ordering, not enum declaration order or hash iteration.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "Intent serialization is schema-versioned and strict: unknown fields, unsupported schema/digest scheme versions, malformed digest strings, invalid qualified identities, duplicate collection keys, or an IntentDigest that does not exactly match the recomputed canonical transcript are hard validation failures before any downstream authorization. Omitted/default serialization forms have one defined canonical meaning; no field may obtain a value from process environment or current time while validating intent bytes.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "Intent and artifact-slot persistence is structurally secret-safe. A private durable-schema inventory maps every serializable field in ReleaseIntentV1 and its nested serializable records exactly once to Decision, TypedWorkspaceObservation, SourceTrustPolicy, StaticSchema, or ComputedCanonicalValue. A two-way coverage test fails both for a serialized field missing from the inventory and for an inventory entry that is no longer serialized. No source variant denotes environment, credential, raw endpoint, raw file bytes, or provider response.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-008",
      "criterion": "callisto-model remains a Layer-1, provider-neutral crate. It may own typed values, parsing/validation, canonical transcript construction, SHA-256, serde, schemars, and miette-compatible error types, but it must not depend on callisto-graph, callisto-vcs, a forge SDK, URL/network clients, or a registry client. Endpoint parsing and prepared remote inputs remain above the model boundary.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "verification": [
    "Golden and permutation tests cover stable canonical intent bytes/digests for reordered input vectors/maps, then mutate one semantic field at a time and assert a digest change.",
    "Negative tests cover raw/unknown fields, duplicate exact operation IDs, same bare name across ecosystems, invalid or cyclic DAGs, a stale/mismatched digest, malformed binding digest, unsafe endpoint forms at the graph-to-model boundary, and registry-binding collision attempts.",
    "Schema/provenance tests assert two-way coverage of all serialized durable fields and confirm endpoint/configuration secrets cannot enter an intent through an untyped extension field."
  ],
  "implementation_notes": [
    "Retain the existing versioned length-prefixed transcript approach rather than introduce JSON canonicalization unless independent cross-language digest recomputation becomes an explicit requirement. The format must be documented and test-vector-backed.",
    "Use BTree collections or defined sorted vectors for durable ordering. If existing enum Ord conflicts with its canonical serialized identity, implement a dedicated canonical sort key; do not rely on declaration order.",
    "Use miette for transient diagnostics and closed safe data structures for persistence. Do not persist miette reports or arbitrary provider errors.",
    "The successor graph-capability spec will construct an opaque non-Serialize/non-Clone ValidatedReleaseIntent from this durable intent, freshly re-observed workspace state, Git evidence, a workspace lock, and operation-specific private prepared inputs."
  ]
}
{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-TRUST-010",
  "status": "draft",
  "owner_track": "release-security",
  "last_verified_revision": "289001ec",
  "depends_on": [
    "SPEC-RELEASE-DECISION-008",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-INTENT-MODEL-009"
  ],
  "purpose": "Define the Git-only source-trust and workspace-exclusion contract used by version-1 durable releases. It turns the approved source commit into freshly observed, non-durable trust evidence and prevents two Callisto release processes from validating or effecting the same workspace concurrently.",
  "scope": "Fresh Git observation, canonical workspace-root binding, clean detached-HEAD policy, local release locking, and re-observation rules. This does not define release policy, durable intent fields, graph preparation, provider effects, or a non-Git release mode.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Supporting branch, symbolic-HEAD, dirty-worktree, non-Git, HermeticContent, or SHA-256 Git-object-format execution in version 1.",
    "Using a Git SHA as evidence of human authorship, signature verification, or forge authorization.",
    "Persisting a workspace path, lock handle, raw git output, remote URL, or arbitrary Git diagnostic in a durable intent, state, receipt, or artifact manifest.",
    "Making an advisory local lock substitute for re-observing Git state before effects."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "Version-1 authorization starts from a caller-supplied workspace root, canonicalizes that root, discovers the Git repository containing it, and rejects a root that does not resolve to that repository's worktree root. The observed identity is the exact full CommitSha at HEAD; it is never supplied by a CLI flag, inferred from a branch name, or obtained from a remote ref. The private trust evidence binds the canonical root and observed commit together.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Version 1 accepts only a SHA-1 Git repository and a detached HEAD at the exact 40-hex GitCommit SourceIdentity in the received ReleaseIntentV1. It rejects SHA-256 Git-object-format repositories, a symbolic HEAD even when it currently resolves to the same commit, an absent/unresolvable HEAD, a non-canonical or non-equal commit, and a shallow/incomplete checkout when the required commit or tag inspection cannot be performed. Equality uses CommitSha values, never prefix or raw-string comparison.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Authorization requires a clean worktree before the capability is issued. 'Clean' means Git reports no staged, unstaged, unmerged, or untracked file under the repository worktree; ignored files do not make it dirty. The ignored-file exception is Git's fixed ignored classification, not a caller-configured path allowlist and not arbitrary .gitignore parsing by Callisto. A path outside the canonical root, an error while determining status, or an ambiguous repository layout fails closed. Diagnostics identify the policy condition and safe relative paths where available, but do not retain raw command output in durable data.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "A ReleaseWorkspaceLock is acquired after root/repository discovery and held from the final successful authorization observation until the validated capability is dropped after state/receipt persistence. Its lock identity is derived from the canonical workspace root using a stable collision-resistant digest and its lock file is outside the checkout in the platform application-state area (or a caller-selected operational directory). It is an exclusive OS-backed advisory lock, releases on process exit/crash, and reports contention with a stable structured error. The lock path and handle are private operational data, not serializable release data.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "The release executor re-observes the same canonical root, detached HEAD, clean-worktree status, and exact approved commit while holding ReleaseWorkspaceLock immediately before every remote or VCS effect. A change after authorization blocks the next effect; the lock only coordinates cooperating Callisto processes and never makes external writers safe by assumption.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "The trust API is a read-only callisto-vcs surface returning closed, typed GitCommitTrustEvidence and ReleaseWorkspaceLock values. It does not depend on callisto-graph, callisto-cli, forge SDKs, or registry clients. Its errors derive miette::Diagnostic with stable codes and remediation, and its durable-facing output contains only validated typed identities rather than shell text.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "Tests cover non-Git roots, nested/non-root invocation, symbolic HEAD, dirty tracked and untracked worktrees, commit mismatch, lock contention, lock release after drop, and a mutation between authorization and an effect-time recheck. Tests assert that each failure occurs before an effect adapter is invoked.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "implementation_notes": [
    "GitAccess/ShellGit should expose structured observation rather than making graph parse porcelain or command text. The existing CommitSha type is the equality boundary.",
    "Use a maintained cross-platform file-lock crate only if it provides process-lifetime exclusive locking with explicit acquisition errors. Keep the dependency in callisto-vcs or a lower permitted layer; do not introduce Tokio or Rayon for this ordered, lock-held path.",
    "A future closed-root byte observer may add HermeticContent under a separate schema/profile version. It must not weaken the GitCommit behavior specified here."
  ],
  "reasoning": "A Git commit only becomes a release trust boundary when the executor proves it is operating at the approved checkout and prevents its own concurrent release processes from racing. A local lock improves coordination; mandatory fresh observation preserves correctness when another tool changes the workspace."
}
{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-CAPABILITY-011",
  "status": "draft",
  "owner_track": "release-security",
  "last_verified_revision": "289001ec",
  "depends_on": [
    "SPEC-RELEASE-DECISION-008",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-INTENT-MODEL-009",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-TRUST-010"
  ],
  "purpose": "Define fresh graph authorization as the only conversion from a durable approved intent into exact, private execution inputs. The resulting capability is deliberately non-transferable and is the authority an effect executor needs in addition to ApplyPermit.",
  "scope": "Root-bound fresh semantic observation, exact intent comparison, release-DAG construction, prepared-operation ownership, and the public/private API boundary in callisto-graph. This spec does not define provider network protocols, durable state/receipts, artifact download, or CLI/workflow command syntax.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Replanning a release from current configuration or silently substituting a current selection for an approved release decision.",
    "Treating PublishPlan, PublishReport, PLAN_JSON, a registry URL supplied at execution time, a recomputed tag, or a mutable config object as effect authority.",
    "Serializing, cloning, caching, or transmitting prepared endpoints, filesystem paths, lock handles, parsed provider clients, credentials, or ValidatedReleaseIntent itself.",
    "Parallel effects or arbitrary operation reordering."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "callisto-graph creates a release observation only from a canonical workspace root while holding the GitCommitTrustEvidence and ReleaseWorkspaceLock required by SPEC-RELEASE-TRUST-010. It re-runs the normal configuration resolution, package discovery, linked-group/cascade/prerelease policy resolution, and dependency analysis from disk; it never trusts a caller-provided workspace, selection, package list, endpoint, source SHA, or previously cached Workspace interior state as fresh evidence.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Validation compares the received ReleaseIntentV1 exactly against the newly observed typed ReleaseDecisionV1 and typed semantic-input snapshot from SPEC-RELEASE-INTENT-MODEL-009. It rejects any difference in source identity, selected/linked-group-expanded roster, target version, prerelease/group/cascade policy, package role/path identity, relevant dependency edges, registry binding identity, tag/forge policy, declared artifact slots, operation identity, prerequisite set, or canonical operation order. It must not compare raw configuration bytes, Display/Debug strings, generic kind/fingerprint pairs, or credential-bearing endpoint text.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "The operation graph is constructed from the approved typed operation identities and explicit prerequisites. It is a DAG by validation; its canonical order is a stable Kahn topological order that always selects the lexicographically smallest ready ReleaseOperationId. A cycle, a missing prerequisite, a duplicate identity, or an operation outside the approved roster is a structured authorization failure. Dependency ordering is never inferred from enum declaration order or hash-map iteration.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "On an exact match, graph returns ValidatedReleaseIntent. This type is defined in callisto-graph, is non-Clone and non-Serialize/non-Deserialize, has no public constructor, and keeps its fields private. It owns the matching ReleaseIntentV1, GitCommitTrustEvidence, ReleaseWorkspaceLock, canonical root, and a complete BTreeMap keyed by ReleaseOperationId of private PreparedOperation values. It may expose the durable intent for inspection but exposes no raw prepared operation value or authority to alter it.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "Each PreparedOperation is created during the same successful fresh observation and contains exactly the effect inputs for its operation: registry operations retain the resolved package directory, exact qualified package/version, parsed and policy-checked endpoint, typed RegistryBindingId, and any source-artifact reference; tag operations retain the exact TagName, approved target CommitSha, and annotation policy/message; forge creation retains the exact tag/target binding; artifact upload retains its exact intent-bound ArtifactSlotId, forge binding, and attestation policy. Endpoint preparation uses a standards-compliant URL parser, rejects userinfo, query, and fragment components, normalizes the approved binding before its digest is compared, and never writes raw endpoint text to any durable type or diagnostic.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "Effect-dispatch APIs accept a ValidatedReleaseIntent reference plus ApplyPermit and select prepared operations only by exact ReleaseOperationId. They neither accept PublishPlan nor consult mutable workspace configuration to reconstruct package paths, endpoints, tags, versions, or prerequisites. A capability can be used only while its lock/trust evidence remains valid; the executor invokes the effect-time recheck from SPEC-RELEASE-TRUST-010 before dispatching each operation.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "Validation failures are stable GraphError variants with miette diagnostics that identify the safe semantic field or operation identity that changed. They do not include credentials, endpoint text, raw configuration, arbitrary provider output, or a debug rendering of a prepared operation. A mismatch yields no capability and no effect adapter invocation.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-008",
      "criterion": "Tests use independently constructed workspaces and fake effect dispatchers to prove rejection for changed manifest/config/policy/selection/DAG/source/registry-binding/artifact-slot inputs, endpoint credentials/query/fragments, duplicate or cyclic operations, and source substitution. Tests prove a valid intent retains prepared inputs from the observed workspace and that every rejected validation path makes zero adapter calls; they also prove stable topological order across differently inserted equivalent input collections.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "implementation_notes": [
    "Keep durable semantic types in callisto-model and discovery/preparation in callisto-graph to preserve the license/dependency direction. callisto-model must not depend on callisto-graph.",
    "Use BTreeMap/BTreeSet and explicit Ord implementations for durable and scheduling order. Do not use PackageId::matches for release-operation identity: it intentionally supports a bare-name wildcard and is not an exact authorization predicate.",
    "Workspace currently contains interior caches. Fresh authorization should construct or invalidate an observation boundary rather than claiming a pre-existing cached graph is current."
  ],
  "reasoning": "The durable intent records what was approved. It is intentionally not sufficient to perform effects: filesystem locations, endpoints, and clients are mutable operational inputs. A graph-private, freshly observed capability bridges that gap without serializing secrets or allowing an old plan path to regain authority."
}
{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-012",
  "status": "draft",
  "owner_track": "release-security",
  "last_verified_revision": "289001ec",
  "depends_on": [
    "SPEC-RELEASE-INTENT-MODEL-009",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-TRUST-010",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-CAPABILITY-011"
  ],
  "purpose": "Execute one freshly validated release intent safely across registries, Git tags, and forge releases. Execution records durable progress before each effect, recovers by observing exact provider identities, and accepts compiled binaries only through an intent-bound artifact manifest plus a verified GitHub artifact attestation.",
  "scope": "The provider-neutral execution coordinator, durable state and terminal receipt, state storage, reconciliation, artifact-manifest validation, and provider observation/effect adapter contracts. It consumes the approved decision, typed intent, Git trust evidence, and ValidatedReleaseIntent defined by its dependencies. It does not define the CLI or GitHub Actions invocation surfaces; SPEC-RELEASE-INTERFACES-013 owns those.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Distributed transactions, rollback of a completed registry publication, or parallel remote effects.",
    "Publishing, tagging, or creating a forge release from PublishPlan, PublishReport, inline configuration, a cache entry, mutable workspace configuration, or a caller-computed provider target.",
    "Persisting credentials, raw registry endpoints, request headers, command lines, provider stderr, arbitrary error text, miette reports, or raw configuration bytes in execution state, receipts, or artifact manifests.",
    "Supporting non-Git or non-detached source trust in version 1.",
    "Using Rayon or Tokio to parallelize lock-held, ordered release effects."
  ],
  "terms": {
    "terminal_success": "A role-specific observation proves the exact intended effect exists, or an adapter has just completed that effect and observed it. It is not inferred from the absence of a failure.",
    "terminal_non_success": "A closed, credential-free failure or operator-required outcome. It prevents dependent operations from dispatching.",
    "prepared_operation": "Graph-private, operation-specific input retained by ValidatedReleaseIntent during fresh validation. It is the only source from which an adapter may construct an effect request.",
    "artifact_slot": "A typed output requested by an intent for a compiled binary release, including its package/version/platform/role identity and GitHub attestation policy but never arbitrary user file paths.",
    "reconcile": "A read-only report over current state and exact provider observations. It never writes state and never changes an operation status. `release execute --resume` owns any permitted state transition after presenting the same evidence."
  },
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "ReleaseExecutionStateV1 is an intent-bound, credential-free record with exactly one state entry for every operation in ReleaseIntentV1's canonical DAG. New execution initializes every entry as Pending. Before any remote or VCS effect for an operation, the coordinator changes that entry to Attempting and atomically persists a complete parseable state file. Only after the adapter returns and its role-specific postcondition has been observed may the entry become a terminal outcome. State transitions are monotonic: Pending -> Attempting -> terminal, with no terminal-to-pending rewrite and no second attempt of a terminal-success operation. A crash after the Attempting write and before or during the provider call is therefore distinguishable from an operation that was never started.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "State storage has an explicit path override and a safe local default. Without an override, Callisto derives a platform application-state location from the canonical repository root and IntentDigest; it never creates release state in the checkout. CI and advanced callers may provide an explicit path. Every write uses callisto_model::atomic::atomic_write and includes the matching schema/version and intent digest. An existing state file whose schema, intent digest, operation roster, or immutable operation identity differs from the supplied intent is rejected before any effect. State may be uploaded/downloaded as a workflow artifact, but a cache is never authority for state or intent.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Reconciliation is observation-driven and never treats `no recorded failure`, `Attempting`, `Pending`, a missing receipt, or an HTTP success from a different identity as success. `release reconcile` is read-only: Pending remains Pending; only an Attempting operation may be reported as proven-success, conflicting, ambiguous, or still-unproven. `release execute --resume` alone may persist the matching legal result after the same observation. Version 1 performs no automatic retry. A terminal non-success prevents dependent operations and requires an operator to create a fresh release attempt under a separately specified retry policy; it is never silently retried. Dependent operations are eligible only when every declared prerequisite is terminal success. Thus an interruption cannot allow tag or forge-release operations to run because unattempted publishes merely lack failures.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "The coordinator dispatches effects in the intent's canonical topological order while retaining the workspace release lock from final capability validation through the last state/receipt persistence. Before each dispatch it rechecks the Git trust evidence, intent binding, artifact binding when applicable, and prerequisite terminal-success states while holding that lock. A changed HEAD, non-detached checkout, dirty tree, missing/mismatched prepared operation, stale intent, or failed prerequisite aborts before that effect. The lock coordinates Callisto processes; the rechecks protect against external writers and provider races.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "Provider adapters accept only (&ValidatedReleaseIntent, &ApplyPermit, OperationId) or a narrower internal capability derived from those values; they do not accept PublishPlan, free-form package/version/URL/tag arguments, or mutable workspace config. A registry observation/effect is keyed by the exact ReleasePackageId, target version, and RegistryBindingId. A Git tag observation/effect verifies the exact tag name, target commit, annotated-versus-lightweight policy, and required annotation policy. A forge-release observation/effect verifies the exact forge binding and tag/target identity. An identity collision, an already-existing object with different metadata, or an ambiguous provider response is a safe non-success outcome, never an idempotent success.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "ArtifactManifestV1 is a typed, credential-free build output bound to exactly one IntentDigest and the intent's exact source commit. It contains one entry for each required artifact slot and, for each entry, the declared slot identity, normalized platform/role, raw-byte SHA-256 ArtifactDigest, byte length, and safe GitHub attestation locator/subject digest. Before any binary forge-upload operation, the executor validates the manifest's schema, intent digest, source commit, complete unique slot roster, and downloaded bytes; it then verifies the exact downloaded bytes with GitHub artifact-attestation verification, pins the intent's configured repository and immutable signer workflow, and checks the verified provenance statement's source commit against the intent. A signature alone, a matching byte digest alone, or an attestation from another repository/workflow/source commit is insufficient. Missing, duplicate, unexpected, wrong-platform, wrong-version, wrong-source, digest-mismatched, unattested, or policy-mismatched assets block the relevant operation. Version 1 rejects binary slots outside this GitHub-attested path; source registry publishing remains a distinct operation and does not consume or imply a compiled-binary artifact.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "After every operation reaches a terminal outcome, the coordinator persists state before evaluating the next operation. A ReleaseReceiptV1 is written only after every operation is terminal success and after final source/intent binding rechecks; it records the intent digest, safe terminal evidence, and receipt schema/version. A partially completed or non-successful release has state but no successful terminal receipt. Receipt creation is idempotent only for byte-equivalent receipt identity; a mismatched existing receipt is rejected.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-008",
      "criterion": "All persisted outcome/error fields are closed enums and fixed safe identifiers. User-facing failures use miette diagnostics with actionable remediation, but diagnostics are transient and never serialized as durable evidence. Tests cover interruption immediately before a provider call, immediately after a provider-side success but before terminal persistence, already-existing exact identity, already-existing conflicting identity, stale state, changed Git evidence, artifact-manifest substitution, and an unattempted prerequisite; each test asserts no unauthorized dependent effect dispatches.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "implementation_notes": [
    "callisto-graph owns the ReleaseCoordinator and provider observation/effect adapter traits. callisto-vcs owns only the Git trust/effect adapter. callisto-cli assembles adapters and maps diagnostics; it does not own release policy or state transitions.",
